//! Mobile-friendly interface to the Mumcord engine, with Kotlin and Swift bindings generated by
//! UniFFI (proc-macro mode).
//!
//! Shape: commands go in as methods of [`MumcordEngine`] (server list, connecting, channels,
//! people, settings), state comes out as a [`BridgeSnapshot`] and as [`BridgeEvent`]s delivered
//! to the app's [`EngineListener`]. The app
//! hands in its secure storage as a [`SecureStorage`]. Engine types never appear in the
//! bindings; the mapping lives in `convert`.
//!
//! # Chat
//!
//! Message content crosses the bridge as [`MessageContent`], a small closed model (text runs
//! with a fixed set of styles, checked `http`/`https` links, quotes, code, images by reference),
//! never as HTML: the engine sanitises what the server sends, and the bridge keeps only what the
//! model knows, so an app cannot be made to interpret markup. History is read in pages
//! ([`MumcordEngine::chat_page`]), new messages arrive as [`BridgeEvent::ChatMessageReceived`],
//! image bytes are fetched on demand, and [`BridgeEvent::ChatNotification`] names the messages
//! worth a system notification. Names, message text and addresses are never logged.
//!
//! # Identities and the transfer from the desktop
//!
//! An identity is the certificate the user is known by on servers. Certificate and key live
//! only in the app's [`SecureStorage`]; the bridge hands out an [`Identity`] (label, common
//! name, fingerprint, date, default flag) and takes or gives key material only as the bytes of
//! a password-protected `.p12` file ([`MumcordEngine::import_identity`],
//! [`MumcordEngine::export_identity`]). The list is part of the snapshot and changes arrive as
//! [`BridgeEvent::IdentitiesChanged`].
//!
//! "Send to your phone" is a [`TransferScan`] ([`MumcordEngine::new_transfer`]): the app feeds
//! it every QR text the camera sees, then the typed code, shows the [`TransferPreview`] and
//! imports. The opened transfer never leaves that object.
//!
//! Until both apps create or bring an identity during their setup
//! ([`MumcordEngine::ensure_default_identity`]), connecting creates a default identity silently
//! when there is none. That shortcut then goes and connecting without an identity fails with
//! [`BridgeError::NoIdentity`].
//!
//! # Phone settings and the public server list
//!
//! [`MobileSettings`] are part of the snapshot and arrive as
//! [`BridgeEvent::MobileSettingsChanged`]: several screens and the platform code (audio route,
//! notifications, network) read them, so they follow the same rule as all other state instead
//! of being fetched by each reader. The public server list is read in pages
//! ([`MumcordEngine::public_servers`]); the engine downloads it only when the app asks.
//!
//! [`AppSettings`] (language, chat, the notification switches) are the settings the phone shares
//! with the desktop's settings model; changes arrive as [`BridgeEvent::AppSettingsChanged`]. A
//! notification switched off there never arrives as [`BridgeEvent::ChatNotification`].
//!
//! # Profile, moderation and the activity log
//!
//! [`MumcordEngine::person_profile`] reads a person's comment (in the chat's safe content model)
//! and avatar; the user edits their own on the connected server, and
//! [`BridgeEvent::ProfileChanged`] says when a shown profile should be read again. Moderation
//! (move, server mute and deafen, kick, ban) follows the server's permission answers in
//! [`Channel::permissions`]; the server decides and its refusal comes back as `PermissionDenied`.
//! The activity log of the connection ([`MumcordEngine::activity_log`],
//! [`BridgeEvent::ActivityAdded`]) is kept in memory and ends with the connection.
//!
//! # Themes
//!
//! [`ThemeSettings`] (appearance mode, app theme, the user's own themes, the servers' themes)
//! come from [`MumcordEngine::theme_settings`] and arrive again as [`BridgeEvent::ThemesChanged`].
//! The app draws with [`ThemeTokens`] ([`MumcordEngine::theme_tokens`]), the colours the
//! desktop derives from a theme's key colours. Theme codes are the desktop's, byte for byte: a
//! pasted code is previewed ([`MumcordEngine::theme_code_preview`]), then imported; codes are
//! untrusted input, size-capped and read as colours and plain text only.
//!
//! # Crash reports
//!
//! Crash reports work as on the desktop and follow the same privacy rules: the engine saves a
//! report for a panic or a fatal signal on the phone (version, system, cause, stack, the redacted
//! last log lines; the home folder shortened to `~`), and nothing is sent until the user sends
//! one report themselves. [`crash_install`] starts it at app start, before the engine exists;
//! [`crash_take_unseen`] names the reports for the notice after a crash, [`crash_list`],
//! [`crash_read`] and the delete functions serve the crash list, and
//! [`MumcordEngine::crash_upload`] sends one report after the user saw
//! [`crash_upload_preview`] and confirmed it.
//!
//! # Threads
//!
//! The bridge owns one tokio runtime. `async` methods run on it and never block the caller.
//! [`MumcordEngine::new`] opens the database and reads the secure storage, so apps call it off
//! the main thread. Events arrive in order on one bridge thread (`mumcord-bridge-events`), never
//! on an audio thread. [`SecureStorage`] is called from the constructor's thread and from
//! runtime threads.
//!
//! ## Calling rule for host apps
//!
//! A waiting command is finished by a runtime thread (`mumcord-bridge`), which calls the
//! generated bindings' continuation callback. The host must not let its own code continue on
//! that thread:
//!
//! - Kotlin: never await a command from a coroutine that resumes where it is completed (no
//!   dispatcher, `Dispatchers.Unconfined`). The coroutine would continue inside the callback and
//!   poll the finished future there, which calls back a second time inside the first. JNA attached
//!   the thread to the Java runtime only for the callback and detaches it when the inner one
//!   returns, while the outer one is still running Kotlin code. Android ends the process for
//!   that (`attempting to detach while still running code`); no exception can be caught. Run
//!   every command on a dispatcher of the app's own and hand the result to the caller from there
//!   (Android: `EngineCalls`).
//! - Swift: a continuation is never resumed in place, the task goes back to its executor, so
//!   awaiting a command is safe from anywhere.
//! - Both: [`EngineListener::on_event`] runs on the event thread. It must return quickly and
//!   only queue the event; it must not call the engine and must not wait for anything.
//!
//! # Audio
//!
//! The app captures and plays the audio itself (only the system's voice-communication session
//! gives echo cancellation and call routing) and moves it through four C functions that its
//! real-time audio threads call directly: `bridge/include/mumcord_audio.h`, with the handles of
//! [`MumcordEngine::audio_endpoints`]. [`AudioWanted`] ([`BridgeEvent::AudioWantedChanged`],
//! [`BridgeSnapshot::audio`]) says when to open the microphone and the speaker.
//!
//! # Streams
//!
//! Screen shares and cameras in the user's channel are listed in [`BridgeSnapshot::streams`] and
//! [`BridgeEvent::StreamsChanged`]; [`MumcordEngine::watch_stream`] fetches one. The engine
//! decodes the video (VP9 with libvpx, AV1 with dav1d) and keeps the newest I420 picture per
//! stream; [`VideoSink`] says when one is ready, and the app's render thread takes it with two
//! C functions (`bridge/include/mumcord_video.h`, handle of [`MumcordEngine::video_handle`]) and
//! uploads the planes straight from the bridge's memory.
//!
//! # Camera
//!
//! The user's own camera is shared by the engine (VP9, as on the desktop) from pictures the app
//! captures: [`MumcordEngine::set_camera_capture`] gives the engine the app's camera, which it
//! starts with [`MumcordEngine::open_camera_preview`] and stops when sharing ends. The app's
//! camera thread pushes every picture with a C function (`bridge/include/mumcord_camera.h`,
//! handle of [`MumcordEngine::camera_handle`]); [`BridgeEvent::OwnCameraChanged`] tells the state.
//!
//! # Android start-up
//!
//! Checking certificates against the system store needs the app context once per process, before
//! the first [`MumcordEngine`] is created. UniFFI cannot pass a JNI context, so this is a plain
//! JNI function next to the generated bindings. The app declares
//!
//! ```kotlin
//! package app.mumcord.mobile.platform.securestore
//!
//! object SystemCertificates {
//!     external fun init(context: android.content.Context)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! loads the library (`System.loadLibrary("mumcord_mobile")`) and calls
//! `SystemCertificates.init(applicationContext)`. The app also has to ship the Kotlin companion
//! library of `rustls-platform-verifier` (Maven artifact `org.rustls:rustls-platform-verifier`
//! in the version of the `rustls-platform-verifier-android` crate, from the Maven repository
//! named in that crate's README). iOS needs no start-up call.
//!
//! # Bindings
//!
//! ```text
//! cargo build -p mumcord-mobile-bridge
//! cargo run -p mumcord-mobile-bridge --features bindgen --bin uniffi-bindgen -- generate \
//!     --library <target dir>/debug/libmumcord_mobile.so --language kotlin --out-dir <dir>
//! ```
//!
//! and the same with `--language swift`. Kotlin package `app.mumcord.mobile.bridge`, Swift
//! module `MumcordBridge` (`uniffi.toml`).

